 "captions": {
  "script": "This lake is pink. Not at sunset. In broad daylight. Glitch Diary, entry thirteen. Lake Hillier, on an island off Western Australia. It's about six hundred metres long. Just a thin strip of sand and trees keeps it from the deep blue ocean. Pink, right next to blue. Like a bug in the colour settings. But that's not the strange part. Scoop the water into a glass... and it stays pink. The colour is in the water itself. And for a long time, nobody knew exactly why... Now we do. The lake is about as salty as the Dead Sea. Only tiny life survives there. Salt-loving algae and bacteria make red and pink pigments, like sunscreen. Billions of them tint the whole lake. Would you swim in a pink lake? Tell me in the comments. Follow for the next entry of my glitch diary. Because this lake is pink...",
